    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, that's the thing. You know, this is what I've been fighting for now, my entire career, the new economy, the new jobs, reshoring from China and other countries and really, you know, to have a Taiwanese company like Foxconn investing a significant amount of money into the Maloney Valley would be huge. You know, doing electric vehicles, both trucks and cars across the street, the General Motors battery plant, that's 1100 full-time union jobs once that's up and running. So that voltage valley, the whole idea of the new ways of manufacturing, next generation manufacturing are huge. And then you throw in the Intel project, which is going to end up being, when it's all said, about $100 billion, average wage rate, In that fab, they call it a fab. They used to call it a factory, but now it's called a fab. $135,000 a year. The least paid person at the fab is going to make $100,000 a year. So these new jobs are going to be huge and then 7,000 union construction jobs to build it, plus the infrastructure. So this is where we need to be and we just got to hit the gas pedal and keep going this way. They're also talking about bringing 30 or 40 suppliers into Ohio because they want to be around the fat. That's where you're going to see Akron, Canton, Youngstown, and hopefully other places like Steubenville and Portsmouth that can land one of these suppliers, supply chain companies, tier one, tier two, tier three. Because two, three, four hundred jobs in a small community can go a hell of a long way to pay for police, pay for fire, pay for schools. So this is huge. We just got it. We need more of it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
